  "body": "The mechanism treats the delivery bag as an unordered multiset of immutable events. First, booking identities are deduplicated with unique over the booking field of every event, so replay and duplicate delivery cannot create extra bookings. For each booking, the latest revision is selected with max_by on the integer revision field. Because a booking/revision pair never conflicts, any duplicate of the latest revision is harmless: max_by returns one of them and their contents agree. The latest event is then classified: if kind is \"hold\" and expires_at > now, its quantity is taken; otherwise (release, or expired hold) it contributes zero. This is why expiry and later cancellation interact correctly: expiry is tested only on the latest event, and a later release simply becomes the latest revision and zeroes the booking, without reviving any older unexpired hold. Quantity is used as the total for that booking version, never as a delta. The resulting per-booking contributions are item-quantity pairs. For each stock item, names are taken from input.stock and sorted ascending, so unused items are included. Reserved is the sum of quantities of contributions matching that item; available is capacity minus reserved, never clamped. Assumptions: booking/revision contents are immutable and non-conflicting; item is immutable per booking; events only name listed stock items; hold events carry expires_at and quantity, while release events may omit them (the short-circuiting and if test avoids reading missing fields). A falsifying observation would be a same-booking same-revision pair with conflicting quantities or kinds, an expired latest hold that should somehow revive an older hold, a release that should not cancel, events naming unlisted items, or any demand to clamp negative availability. A revision preserves the successful behavior that output is ascending by item, includes every stock item, and reports reserved as the sum of active latest holds and available as capacity minus reserved under duplicate delivery and arbitrary event order.",
